{"id": 114785, "stem": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-text\"><span><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-100\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">A</mark> 2-week-old girl is brought to the office by her parents for her first check-up.\u00a0 She was born at 37 weeks gestation via an uncomplicated spontaneous vaginal delivery.\u00a0 The mother reports that the patient has been formula-feeding due to a poor latch and does not take a bottle well.\u00a0 Birth weight was 3.6 kg (7 lb 15 oz); weight today is 3.2 kg (7 lb 1 oz).\u00a0 Examination is remarkable for a poor suck, tongue fasciculations, and diffuse hypotonia and hyporeflexia.\u00a0 The patient is admitted to the hospital, and further work-up reveals a deletion in the <em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">SMN1</em> gene.\u00a0 Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of this patient\u2019s condition?\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b</p></span></div>", "options": [{"label": "A", "text": "Anterior horn degeneration"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Cerebral white matter injury"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Neuromuscular junction dysfunction"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Peripheral nerve degeneration"}, {"label": "E", "text": "Skeletal muscle degradation"}], "correctAnswer": "A", "explanation": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-explanation\"><span class=\"\"><div id=\"div_T105088\" style=\"overflow-x: auto; font-size: 1rem;\"><div class=\"group/triggerable\" data-is-open=\"false\"><div class=\"group/draggable\" data-is-draggable=\"true\" data-is-dragging=\"false\"><div class=\"group/resizable relative\" data-is-maximized=\"false\" data-is-resizable=\"true\" data-is-resizing=\"false\"><div class=\"relative max-w-full\"><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-r from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute right-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-l from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div class=\"max-w-full overflow-x-auto [&amp;_td]:min-w-[8rem] [&amp;_th]:min-w-[8rem]\"><table class=\"border bg-transparent dark:text-white border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white hover:bg-transparent warm:hover:bg-transparent dark:hover:bg-transparent cursor-default mt-2 mb-3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tbody style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"2\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-50\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">S</mark>pinal muscular atrophy</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Pathogenesis</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Autosomal recessive deletion in <em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">SMN1</em> gene \u2192 deficiency of SMN protein</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Degeneration of motor neurons (anterior horn cells &amp; lower brainstem nuclei) \u2192 progressive muscle atrophy</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Variants</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Onset, severity, &amp; presenting symptoms vary<ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"list-style-type: circle; font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Severe (infantile, most common): diffuse, flaccid paralysis</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Moderate (toddler): delay/loss of motor skills</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Mild (adolescent): symmetric proximal weakness (legs &gt;arms)</li></ul></li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Examination findings</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Lower motor neuron signs (eg, hypotonia, hyporeflexia, weakness)</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Tongue fasciculations</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Diagnosis</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">SMN1</em> gene analysis</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"2\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">SMN</strong> = survival motor neuron.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div></div></div><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">This patient with severe, generalized weakness has <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">tongue fasciculations</strong> indicative of <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">lower motor neuron disease</strong>.\u00a0 These findings in a <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">newborn</strong> are suggestive of <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">spinal muscular atrophy</strong> (SMA), which is confirmed by this patient's genetic testing.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">SMA is an autosomal recessive, lower motor neuron disease most commonly caused by an exon <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">deletion</strong> within the <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">SMN1</em> gene</strong>.\u00a0 <em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">SMN1</em> codes for SMN (survival motor neuron) protein, which is essential for maintaining the survival and functionality of lower motor neurons of the spinal cord and brainstem.\u00a0 Loss of SMN protein in patients with SMA results in <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">degeneration</strong> of:</p><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start inline\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">The <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-gq75s5u48nef4w1x86bkho3f\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-gq75s5u48nef4w1x86bkho3f\">anterior horn</a> of the spinal cord, which leads to diminished strength, hypotonia, and hyporeflexia.</p></li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start inline\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Lower cranial nerve motor nuclei</strong>, which manifests as tongue fasciculations and swallow dysfunction due to involvement of the hypoglossal (CN XII) and glossopharyngeal (CN IX) nuclei, respectively.</p></li></ul><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">Infantile SMA (Werdnig-Hoffmann disease) is the most common type and presents in the first few months of life with <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">diffuse flaccid paralysis</strong> and <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">failure to thrive</strong>, as seen in this case.\u00a0 Respiratory failure and a short lifespan are expected due to progressive respiratory muscle weakness.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice B)</strong>\u00a0 Cerebral white matter injury is often seen in infants with cerebral palsy, which can initially present with hypotonia before classic upper motor neuron signs (eg, spasticity) develop.\u00a0 However, tongue fasciculations are indicative of lower motor neuron disease, not an upper motor neuron lesion.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice C)</strong>\u00a0 Neuromuscular junction dysfunction (eg, myasthenia gravis) typically presents with fluctuating and fatigable weakness.\u00a0 In contrast to this patient's presentation, reflexes are typically preserved, and tongue fasciculations do not occur.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice D)</strong>\u00a0 Peripheral nerve degeneration, as seen in Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease, causes lower motor neuron findings and sensory deficits.\u00a0 However, slowly progressive distal limb weakness is typical rather than flaccid paralysis in the neonatal period, and cranial nerve involvement (eg, tongue fasciculations) is rare.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice E)</strong>\u00a0 Conditions primarily due to degradation of skeletal muscle (eg, muscular dystrophy) cause weakness, hypotonia, and hyporeflexia (from muscle wasting) but not tongue fasciculations.\u00a0 Although muscle atrophy occurs with SMA, it is due to denervation from anterior horn degeneration rather than primary muscle pathology.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Educational objective:</strong>Spinal muscular atrophy is a lower motor neuron disorder that should be suspected in a newborn with flaccid paralysis and tongue fasciculations.\u00a0 Pathogenesis involves an <em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">SMN1</em> gene deletion resulting in loss of motor neurons in the anterior horn cells of the spinal cord and cranial nerve motor nuclei of the lower brainstem.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b</p></span></div>", "educationalObjective": "Spinal muscular atrophy is a lower motor neuron disorder that should be suspected in a newborn with flaccid paralysis and tongue fasciculations.  Pathogenesis involves an SMN1 gene deletion resulting in loss of motor neurons in the anterior horn cells of the spinal cord and cranial nerve motor nuclei of the lower brainstem.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b", "subject": "Pathophysiology", "system": "Nervous System", "topic": "Spinal muscular atrophy", "category": "Congenital and developmental anomalies", "peerCorrect": 61.0}